 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Precision Loss:** The low resolution ($128 \times 32 \times 128$) may lead to inaccurate distance estimates for thin cloud features, potentially causing the ray to skip over small details or intersect the surface prematurely [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Boundary Conditions:** The vertical wrapping mode is `GL_CLAMP_TO_EDGE`, which must be handled correctly in texture coordinate mapping to avoid artifacts at the top and bottom of the cloud volume [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Synchronization:** The bake shader must be dispatched before the rendering shader samples the texture. This is managed by dispatching in `AtmosphereManager::Update` to solve initialization frame order issues [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Unadvected Coordinates:** The baking uses unadvected coordinates, meaning the texture represents the cloud in its local space. The raymarching loop must correctly transform the ray position to this local space before sampling [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Precision:** The cached-distance error is $\le$ half a voxel diagonal near the surface [S2, unofficial]. This must be verified by sampling against the true DE [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory:** Brick count must be capped to control memory usage [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Synchrony:** In dynamic scenes, the bake shader must be dispatched every frame to ensure synchrony with dynamic cloud parameters [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Initialization:** Dispatching the bake shader every frame also solves initialization frame order issues [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Bake time must be less than a few frames' worth of computation to be beneficial [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources. The baking step is executed every frame [S1, unofficial]. The raymarching loop is accelerated by skipping empty space, reducing the number of analytical SDF evaluations [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The texture requires $128 \times 32 \times 128 \times 4$ bytes $\approx 2$ MB of VRAM [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** The method was validated with unit tests and shader validations passing 100% [S1, unofficial]. It optimizes cloud sphere tracing by reducing the computational load of the analytical SDF evaluation during the main rendering pass [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Reduces the number of expensive analytical SDF evaluations per ray. Simple to implement as a texture lookup.
-*   **Limitations:** Introduces a fixed memory overhead. Requires frequent rebaking for dynamic clouds.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Baking is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of texels in the 3D texture ($128 \times 32 \times 128 = 524,288$ texels) [S1, unofficial]. Ray marching is accelerated by skipping empty space, reducing the number of expensive analytical DE evaluations [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the 3D texture storage [S1, unofficial]. For brick volumes, memory is proportional to the number of bricks, which is capped [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Interactive frame-time gain measured on User Bulb heavy maps and KIFS, reducing time from seconds to near real time [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:**
+    *   Significant speedup for interactive camera moves (orbit/dolly) over static or slowly changing fractals [S2, unofficial].
+    *   Conservative skipping of empty space reduces ray marching steps [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Works well on weak hardware and heavy maps on strong hardware [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:**
+    *   Baked SDF is a proxy; accuracy is bounded by texture resolution (preview quality) [S2, unofficial].
+    *   Requires rebuilding the cache when parameters change [S2, unofficial].
+    *   Not suitable for final output if high precision is required; full-detail DE render is needed for final frames [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Interactive preview, orbit/dolly camera moves over static/slowly changing volumes, weak hardware, heavy fractal maps [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Final offline rendering (use full DE), highly dynamic volumes where baking overhead exceeds savings, high-precision requirements [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method was introduced in a pull request by `ricecake` to the `boidsish` repository on GitHub, dated July 11, 2026 [S1, unofficial]. It is part of an effort to optimize cloud sphere tracing by leveraging a low-resolution 3D SDF texture as a conservative proxy [S1, unofficial].
+This technique is inspired by methods borrowed from games, offline renderers, and GPU computing [S2, unofficial]. It was filed as Phase 3b of issue #1298 in the context of a 2026-10-17 GPU/rendering performance survey [S2, unofficial]. The specific implementation for cloud sphere tracing was contributed by @ricecake in July 2026 [S1, unofficial]. The sparse distance-field cache concept for interactive 3D camera moves was proposed by AloneButUnsober in October 2026 [S2, unofficial].
 
